<h2>The appeal and the application</h2>
<p>The appellant was convicted in Sessions Case No. 476/2018 and sentenced to seven years&rsquo; imprisonment under Section 307 IPC and five years under Section 394 IPC, running concurrently, together with fines of which Rs 75,000 was directed to the complainant as compensation. He appealed to the High Court under Section 415(2) read with Section 528 BNSS and sought suspension of sentence under Section 430 BNSS — the provision that now houses what practitioners long knew as Section 389 CrPC.</p>
<p>The nominal roll showed custody of three years, eleven months and twenty-six days as of 9 November 2026 — more than half the seven-year term, before even counting remission.</p>

<h2>The governing principles</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Fixed term = liberal approach.</strong> Following <em>Bhagwan Rama Shinde Gosai v. State of Gujarat</em>, (1999) 4 SCC 421, courts consider suspension of fixed-term sentences liberally unless exceptional circumstances exist.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Appeal must stay meaningful.</strong> The Court noted recent Supreme Court authority reiterating that where the appeal cannot realistically be heard before a substantial part of the sentence is served, suspension protects the appeal from becoming academic.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Half the sentence served.</strong> Custody approaching four years against a seven-year maximum weighed decisively; the arithmetic of time served is often the most persuasive page in a suspension application.</div>
</div>

<h2>Suspension of sentence in Delhi: the working checklist</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Element</th><th>What the Court looks at</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Nature of conviction</td><td>Fixed term or life; fixed-term convictions attract the liberal rule</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Time served</td><td>Proportion of sentence already undergone, including remission earned</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prospect of early hearing</td><td>Realistic listing horizon of the appeal in the High Court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conduct and antecedents</td><td>Jail conduct, interim bail record, other cases</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conditions</td><td>Bond, travel restrictions, witness no-contact, reporting</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The order also carries a procedural point worth noting: the Court requisitioned the trial court record in digitised form. Digitised TCRs have quietly shortened the life cycle of criminal appeals in Delhi, removing a step that once added months between admission and hearing.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises an order of the High Court of Delhi dated 26 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and it expresses no view on the pending appeal.</div>
